    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Standard advice on curtains assumes a generous stretch of wall above and beside every window. Real homes, and rentals in particular, are rarely that obliging. The window might run right up to the ceiling, sit tight against a corner, or fill most of the wall. It might be a bay with three or five angled panes. And you might not be allowed to drill at all.</p>
<p>This guide covers the practical options when you need a curtain rod for a window with no wall space, when you want to avoid drilling, and when you are dressing a bay window as a renter.</p>

<h2 id="before-you-buy">Before you buy anything</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check your tenancy agreement.</strong> Some agreements allow existing fixings to be reused or new ones to be added with permission. Ask your landlord or agent and keep their answer in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Look for what is already there.</strong> Old screw holes, a pelmet board or an existing track may let you fit a new pole without making fresh holes.</li>
<li><strong>Identify the window frame.</strong> uPVC, painted timber and aluminium frames behave very differently with clamps and adhesives, and drilling into a uPVC frame can affect the seals and any warranty, so it should be avoided.</li>
<li><strong>Measure the recess.</strong> Note the width and height inside the reveal at the top, middle and bottom, because older windows are often not perfectly square.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="no-wall-space-above">When there is no wall space above the window</h2>
<p>If the window head is flush with the ceiling, or there is only a few centimetres of wall, a conventional pole on brackets will not fit. The cleanest answer is usually a ceiling-mounted track.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ceiling tracks</strong> fix directly to the ceiling and let curtains fall from the very top of the room, which makes ceilings feel taller. They need fixings, so ask your landlord first; a few small holes in the ceiling are often easier to fill and repaint than holes in a reveal.</li>
<li><strong>Ceiling brackets for poles</strong> are an alternative if you prefer the look of a pole.</li>
<li><strong>Choose a track that can bend</strong> or be cut to length if the window sits close to a corner.</li>
</ul>
<!--figure:double-height-sheer-curtains|Tall sheer curtains running the full height of a double-height living room, the effect a ceiling-level fixing creates.-->
<p>Make sure the track will clear any window handles and that the curtains will not foul a radiator below. Curtains should not drape over a radiator, both for heat efficiency and so they stay clear of the heat source.</p>

<h2 id="no-wall-space-beside">When there is no wall space beside the window</h2>
<p>Where a window meets a corner or an adjoining wall, there is nowhere for curtains to stack back when open. Options include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stacking to one side only.</strong> A single wide curtain that draws fully to the side with more space looks deliberate and keeps the glass clear.</li>
<li><strong>Using a lightweight fabric.</strong> Sheers and unlined linen stack much more compactly than heavy lined curtains.</li>
<li><strong>Fitting inside the recess.</strong> A rod inside the reveal avoids the side walls altogether, though the curtains will cover part of the glass when open.</li>
<li><strong>Swapping to a blind.</strong> A roller or Roman blind inside the recess, paired with a single dress curtain, can solve very tight spaces.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="tension-rods">Tension rods inside the recess</h2>
<p>A tension rod is the simplest no-drill option. It is spring-loaded or screw-adjusted to press against the two sides of the window reveal.</p>
<ul>
<li>Best for lightweight curtains, café curtains and sheers.</li>
<li>Choose a rod rated for the curtain weight and the recess width; long rods sag more, so check the manufacturer’s maximum span.</li>
<li>Use rubber end caps to protect paintwork and test on a hidden spot first, as pressure can mark soft paint.</li>
<li>Recesses must be solid and parallel; tension rods will slip in shallow or tapering reveals.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="no-drill-brackets">No-drill brackets that grip the frame</h2>
<p>If you are searching for a curtain rod with no drilling into the window frame, look at clamp-on and self-adhesive brackets.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clamp brackets</strong> hook over the top of the window frame or grip the casement and hold a slim rod. They suit certain tilt-and-turn and casement windows. Window-frame compatibility is everything: check the bracket’s stated frame depth and profile against your window, and make sure the window still opens and closes properly.</li>
<li><strong>Self-adhesive brackets</strong> stick to the frame or wall. They work for light sheers and voiles on smooth, clean surfaces. Follow the weight rating, test removal on a hidden spot, and avoid them for heavy lined or blackout curtains.</li>
<li><strong>Do not hang heavy curtains from adhesive fixings.</strong> If they let go, the rod and fabric come down together.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="bay-window-curtains">Bay window curtains for renters</h2>
<p>Bay windows are beautiful but tricky. Here are the main approaches, roughly from most to least involved.</p>
<h3>Flexible bay track</h3>
<p>A bendable bay track follows the angles of the bay and lets curtains travel all the way around. It gives the neatest result, but it needs ceiling or wall fixings, so it is one to discuss with your landlord.</p>
<h3>Separate rods per pane</h3>
<p>Fitting a short rod or tension rod across each section of the bay is often the most renter-friendly approach. Each section gets its own pair or single panel, so you can open one pane and leave the others covered. Use tension rods where the bay has solid reveals between panes.</p>
<h3>Curtains across the bay opening</h3>
<p>A single straight pole across the mouth of the bay leaves the bay itself open as a nook. It is simpler to fit and suits small bays, but you lose the light-filtering effect at the glass.</p>
<h3>Blinds in each pane, dress curtains at the ends</h3>
<p>Blinds inside each section control light and privacy, while two stationary dress curtains at the outer edges soften the bay.</p>
<!--figure:fluted-walls-sheer-curtains-lounge|Floor-length sheer curtains soften a lounge with fluted walls; light sheers like these suit flexible tracks and slim rods.-->

<h2 id="measuring">How to measure</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Curtain width:</strong> total fabric width of around 1.5–2 times the window or track width gives a full, gathered look. Sheers can go fuller, towards 2–2.5 times.</li>
<li><strong>Rod length:</strong> extend the rod 15–25 cm (6–10 in) beyond each side of the window when space allows, so curtains stack off the glass and let in more light.</li>
<li><strong>Drop:</strong> for floor-length curtains, measure from the track or rod to the floor and aim to finish about 1 cm (½ in) above it, or let them just kiss the floor. For a slight puddle, add 2–5 cm (1–2 in).</li>
<li><strong>Inside a recess:</strong> measure the exact reveal width and subtract a little for the rod mechanism, following the manufacturer’s guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Bay windows:</strong> measure each section separately along the line where the track or rods will sit, and note each angle if you are ordering a bendable track.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Use a metal tape measure rather than a fabric one, and write down every measurement with a sketch of the window. Bays and older frames are rarely as symmetrical as they look.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="choosing-fabric">Choosing fabric for awkward windows</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sheers and voiles</strong> are light enough for tension rods and adhesive brackets, and stack compactly.</li>
<li><strong>Lined curtains</strong> add privacy and insulation but need sturdy fixings.</li>
<li><strong>Blackout</strong> fabrics are heavy; use them with a properly fixed track or pole.</li>
<li><strong>Layering</strong> a sheer on a tension rod inside the recess with a heavier curtain outside gives flexibility, if you have permission for the outer fixing.</li>
</ul>

<p>Curtains and tension rods move with you, which is useful if you rent; see <a href="/blog/decor-that-moves-well-between-apartments">decor that moves well between apartments</a> and our guide to <a href="/blog/heavy-mirror-and-shelving-rental-no-drilling">heavy mirrors and shelving without drilling</a>. For fabric and style advice, read <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-curtains-for-your-living-room">how to choose curtains for your living room</a>, or explore our <a href="/services/curtains-window-treatments">curtains and window treatments</a> service. To get your windows measured and planned, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
